# Jarvis voice local tier: gemma-4:12b (Google QAT q4_0) on llama.cpp,
# alone on the 12 GB 3060.
#
# !! THIS UNIT IS NOT CURRENTLY INSTALLED. !!  As of 2026-08-04 the live voice
# tier on main is a bare nohup'd process in a login-session scope (PPID 1, user
# claude) — so it does NOT survive a reboot and nothing restarts it if it dies.
# The same is true of architect-tts. Installing both needs the operator's sudo:
#     sudo cp scripts/llama-voice.service scripts/architect-tts.service \
#          /etc/systemd/system/
#     sudo systemctl enable --now llama-voice architect-tts
# Note the operator CANNOT start these from their own account: the binaries and
# models live under /home/claude, so `pkill` gets "Operation not permitted" and
# ExecStart gets exit 126. Run them as user claude, or install the units.
#
# !! --reasoning-budget 0 IS LOAD-BEARING. !!  Gemma 4 emits real
# chain-of-thought — 555 completion tokens of reasoning before a three-word
# answer — and its own template already sets enable_thinking to false, so the
# chat-template-kwarg does NOT stop it. Without --reasoning-budget 0, measured
# TTFT ran 13 s / 39 s / 57 s. This is the exact OPPOSITE of qwen3.5, where
# --reasoning-budget 0 does nothing and the template kwarg is the switch; both
# are set here because they are cheap and the failure is catastrophic.
#
# Why llama.cpp rather than ollama: ollama's qwen3.5 GGUF conversion breaks
# llama.cpp's loader, and its template 500s when a tool result is replayed.
# Why it matters that Gemma's template renders tools into the SYSTEM block:
# that keeps them in the cacheable prefix (measured 6488/6498 prompt tokens
# cached). llama3.1 renders them into the last user message, which moves every
# turn and kills the prefix cache — a full re-prefill, forever.
#
# CARD LAYOUT (operator's rule, 2026-08-04): "the llm should live on its own
# gpu, there should be no sharing of cards with the tts or whisper."
#     cuda:0, Ti 8 GB    whisper + architect-tts   (~2.9 GB used)
#     cuda:1, 3060 12 GB the LLM, alone            (8.1 GB used, 3.8 GB free)
# That split is also why the 9B was faster than the 4B it replaced: the Ti was
# costing ~150 ms of TTFT to whisper contention, not to any llama.cpp floor.
#
# WHY THE 12B. Voice gauntlet, both models on the same prompt, N=5 (tool args
# scored by resolving them through the real matcher against the live library):
#
#                        qwen3.5:9b   gemma-4:12b
#     total                  30/50        44/50
#     open-ended / vibe    2/5, 2/5     5/5, 5/5
#     poisoned history        0/5          3/5
#     recitation trap         1/5          3/5
#
# The 9B's failures were almost all FABRICATION — "Playing Seven Nation Army,
# sir." with no tool call — on the two most common ways music gets asked for.
# The 12B costs +319 ms: 1046 ms speech-end-to-first-audio vs 727 ms, which is
# over the 880 ms target but inside the 1-1.5 s band research calls acceptable.
#
# Keep -c in step with JARVIS_VOICE_LOCAL_CONTEXT_WINDOW on the Pi. Compaction
# sizes the local tier's history against it, and overflowing does not error —
# it silently drops the front of the prompt, tool definitions first, which
# looks exactly like a model too dumb to call tools.
#
# Rollback to qwen3.5:9b: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=1, Qwen3.5-9B-Q4_K_M.gguf,
# --alias qwen3.5:9b, add --chat-template-kwargs '{"enable_thinking":false}',
# and JARVIS_VOICE_LOCAL_MODEL=qwen3.5:9b on the Pi.
[Unit]
Description=Jarvis voice LLM tier (llama.cpp, gemma-4:12b, RTX 3060 12GB)
After=network-online.target

[Service]
User=claude
Environment=CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=1
ExecStart=/home/claude/llama.cpp/build/bin/llama-server \
  -m /home/claude/models/gemma-4-12b-it-qat-q4_0.gguf \
  --alias gemma-4:12b \
  --host 0.0.0.0 --port 11436 \
  -ngl 99 -c 32768 -np 1 \
  -fa on \
  --jinja --chat-template-kwargs '{"enable_thinking":false}' \
  --reasoning-budget 0 \
  --metrics
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
